 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to making permanent provisions for charging a fee for admission to the New York Botanical Garden.",
 "title": "Relating to making permanent provisions for charging a fee for admission to the New York Botanical Garden; repealer",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S4449",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S4449-2019",
     "printNo": "S4449",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 148\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 4449, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend chapter 465 of the laws of 1994, amending chapter\n      285 of the laws of 1891 relating to charging a fee  for  admission\n      to  the New York Botanical Garden, in relation to making permanent\n      certain provisions authorizing the imposition of a  required  fee;\n      and  to  repeal  section 2 of such chapter 465 of the laws of 1994\n      relating thereto\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would permanently authorize the New York Botanical Garden to\ncharge a fee for admission by repealing the expiration date  of  a  1994\nlaw  that  authorized  collection  of admission fees. The bill sponsor's\nmemo indicates the bill is necessary to  put  the  Botanical  Garden  on\nparity  with  other institutions which are not required to appeal to the\nLegislature for permission to charge for admission. Unlike other museums\nand gardens, the New York Botanical Garden was created by a  legislative\nact,  and  the  1891  act  which dedicated public land for the Botanical\nGarden stipulated that the park should be free and open to the public.\n \n  A free Botanical Garden was not sustainable in perpetuity and a  nomi-\nnal  fee  was  authorized  in  the  1990s  to address needed repairs and\nconstruction. A 1994 law  (L.1994,  c.465)  authorized  the  corporation\ncharged with the care of the Botanical Garden to provide for free admis-\nsion  to  the  grounds  or  to adopt a fee policy, provided that any fee\npolicy should conform to the requirements of any grant of  public  funds\nand that primary and secondary schools should be admitted for free under\nrules  set  by the corporation's board. The 1994 law adopted this change\nfor one year, after which the 1994 law automatically provided  that  any\nfee charged to the public would be a suggested fee and visitors could be\nallowed to pay a lower or higher amount on their own initiative.\n \n  The  1994  law has been extended periodically, with the implementation\nof a suggested fee to next occur on May 1, 2020. This bill, as  drafted,\nwould make permanent the corporation's authority to charge a fee and the\nonly  justification offered is that the Botanical Garden should not have\nto approach the Legislature for continued permission. Many  state  agen-\ncies and authorities must approach the Legislature for continued author-\nity  to  administer  specific  programs, and often programs necessary to\nprotect public health and the environment must be renewed on  an  annual\nbasis.\n \n  Given the unique conditions on which the New York Botanical Garden was\nestablished,  particularly  the premise that the park should be free and\nopen to the public and a more than 100 year track record of limiting the\nBotanical Garden's authority to charge a fee, it is prudent to veto this\nbill until the Legislature can provide a finding that the  authority  to\ncharge  a  fee  in  perpetuity is necessary to make the Botanical Garden\nfiscally sound. The current  bill  lacks  any  meaningful  analysis  and\ntherefore  provides  insufficient  grounds to overturn the long-standing\npreference to keep this public park free and open to  the  public.  Also\n \nconcerning  is  the lack of analysis on whether the Botanical Garden has\nmade the grounds free for primary and secondary schools, as is  required\nby  the  law. Until it can be demonstrated that the Botanical Garden has\nmet its current obligations, it would be unsound to remove the fee poli-\ncy from state oversight.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
